The original does a linear scan of the whole profile table on every call. This version builds a small direct-index table once, from the constant `mesgs` data, and uses it to answer lookups.

- **Common case:** most message numbers are below 1024, so a lookup is a single array read.
- **Same result for duplicates:** the table keeps only the first index seen for each number, so it returns the same entry the linear scan would.
- **Larger numbers:** anything 1024 or above, such as manufacturer-specific messages, still uses the original linear scan.
- **Thread safety:** the table is a function-local static, so C++ initializes it safely across threads. It depends only on `mesgs`, so it is not tied to any mutable global state.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    struct Table
    {
        enum { SIZE = 1024 };
        int idx[SIZE];
        Table()
        {
            for (int k = 0; k < SIZE; k++)
                idx[k] = -1;
            for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
            {
                const unsigned n = static_cast<unsigned>(mesgs[i].num);
                if (n < static_cast<unsigned>(SIZE) && idx[n] < 0)
                    idx[n] = i;
            }
        }
    };
    static const Table table;

    if (static_cast<unsigned>(num) < static_cast<unsigned>(Table::SIZE))
    {
        const int i = table.idx[num];
        return (i < 0) ? NULL : &mesgs[i];
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
    {
        if (mesgs[i].num == num)
            return &mesgs[i];
    }

    return NULL;
}
```